Rewrite Medical Markdown Files

This is a script that turns medical textbook chapters, imperfectly converted
from PDF, into tagged `.md` study documents. The output files are formatted
specifically for use by the pipeline listed in

  https://github.com/medical-uc/knowledge-graph-pipeline

That pipeline builds a knowledge graph, and textbook prose is poor input for
it. A textbook sentence is written to be read in order by a person: it packs
several claims together, leans on pronouns to name its subject, and leaves a
good deal of its information in tables and diagrams, which are not sentences
at all. So the script rewrites rather than carries over. Each section is read
whole, the information in it is inventoried, and the section is restated as
standalone declarative sentences, one claim each, every sentence naming its
own subject. Tables and mermaid diagrams are restated into words as well.
Figure and table references survive the rewrite and are anchored to the claim
they illustrate.

The rewrite adds nothing. It restates the section's information and may not
infer, generalise, or bring in outside knowledge. It is also not a summary:
splitting packed sentences apart and naming every subject makes the output
longer than the source, which is expected.

Asking for one claim per sentence does not reliably get it either. Splitting
a source's packed sentences apart should leave a section with more sentences
than it started with, so a section that came back with barely more has not
split anything, whatever its wording suggests. That ratio is measured per
section, against two thresholds rather than one. Below 1.5 the section's
paragraphs are sent back to be split; below 1.2 the section is also reported.
Splitting is worth attempting well before a section is bad enough to be worth
complaining about, and the gap between the two is where most of the work
actually gets done: a result that comes back no better is refused and the
original kept, so the wider net costs calls rather than quality.

What counts as a source sentence matters more than it sounds. The PDF
conversion writes math with spaces around its punctuation, as in
`$1 . 8 \times 10^{-9}$`, so splitting on a period cuts a formula in half and
counts each half as a sentence. It also renders a displayed equation as an
alt-text line of its own and strands the clause introducing it on a colon.
None of those is a claim, and they gather in exactly the formula-dense
sections the ratio judges hardest, so they are excluded from the count. A
section with fewer than ten source claims is not judged at all: the ratio
quantises too coarsely there, failing a six-claim section at seven sentences
and passing it at eight.

A result is refused, and the original kept, when it loses a sentence, a
number or a formula, or when it runs past three times its input in words.
The word bound is deliberate: sentences are not bounded, because multiplying
them is the point of the pass and one packed sentence can legitimately
become five.

Formulae are masked before that call and restored after it. Told to copy
`$\mathsf{H}_3\mathsf{O}^+$` across verbatim, the model unwraps it into bare
text, doubles the backslash in `\times`, and drops a closing delimiter so the
span swallows the sentences behind it, all of which corrupt the claim while
leaving every word in place. Each formula therefore goes out as an opaque
`[FORMULA1]` and comes back in afterwards, which removes the opportunity
rather than arguing about it. A result is still refused when a placeholder
does not restore. Sections that read short after all this are reported.

Asking for standalone sentences does not reliably get them. The model still
opens perhaps one sentence in ten with a back-reference such as "This
condition" or "These groups", which names nothing on its own. Those are
found by inspection rather than by asking, and only the paragraphs holding
one are sent back, with the section as context, to have the name filled in.
Any that survive are reported. Repair runs after splitting, because splitting
a sentence in two is itself a way to create a back-reference.

Because the body is rewritten, there is no verbatim text to check the output
against. Coverage is checked instead by a second pass that reads each
section's source against its rewrite and reports the information the rewrite
dropped. Findings are printed as warnings on stderr and the run exits
non-zero. Pass `--no-audit` to skip that pass, which roughly halves the number
of calls. Treat the findings as sections worth reading rather than as a
defect list. The check is itself a model, and it reports information as
missing that the rewrite in fact carries in different words: across the five
Harper's chapters, roughly three findings in four name something the output
demonstrably contains. Several of the rest are transitions and analogies the
rewrite is meant to drop.

Everything outside the body still comes from the chapter itself: the title,
objectives, summary, glossary and reading list are collected, not generated.
`<topic>` is the one element with no source counterpart, and is inferred.

The back end is hosted on

  http://jupyter-02.aml1.id.iosda.org:8888

through vLLM running the model `huatuogpt-o1-72b-4bit`. The Hugging Face path
of the model is `mlx-community/HuatuoGPT-o1-72B-4bit`. There is no offline
mode: a chapter of N sections, T tables, D figure-attached diagrams or grids,
S paragraphs needing splitting and R needing repair costs roughly
2N + T + D + S + R + 1 calls, and without a back end there is no document to
build.
The calls fan out over `--workers`, and `--cache-dir` makes a rerun free.

The file `TAG_SCHEMA.txt` specifies all the tags and what each of them
contains. Regenerate it with `python3 rewrite_medical_md.py --print-schema`.

To run it, simply use:

  python3 rewrite_medical_md.py raw_document.md \
    -o rewritten_document.md \
    -v \
    --cache-dir ./artifacts/rewrite_cache

To process multiple documents at once, use the `batch_process.sh` script. Put
the input documents under `./inputs`, organised by subject and book:

  inputs/biochemistry/harpers_illustrated_biochemistry/chapter_1.md

The script walks that tree, mirrors its structure into `./artifacts/outputs`,
and passes any extra arguments straight through, so `./batch_process.sh
--no-audit` runs the whole corpus without the coverage pass.

A chapter that raised verification warnings is reported as `warnings:` and a
chapter that produced no document at all as `FAILED:`, and only the latter
makes the script exit non-zero. Warnings are the normal outcome of a rewrite,
so treating them as failures would mark every run as failed.
